# Netlify response headers for the static site (no build step, so this file is
# the only place caching can be set).
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
# index.html and js/*.js are the application itself and change on every deploy,
# but they ship under stable names — there is no bundler to fingerprint them.
# With Netlify's default caching a returning player keeps running whatever
# JavaScript their browser cached, sometimes for days, with no way to tell:
# the game looks fine, it is just old. That cost a real debugging session — a
# server-side feature had shipped and applied correctly, and the player still
# saw the gate message from the version before it, because the browser never
# re-fetched js/piracy.js.
#
# `no-cache` does NOT mean "don't store". It means "store it, but revalidate
# before using it", so an unchanged file costs a 304 and nothing more, while a
# deploy is picked up on the very next load. For files this small that is the
# right trade.
#
# assets/ is deliberately left alone: sprites, backgrounds and audio are large,
# effectively immutable, and should stay cached hard.

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/js/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/css/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
